README-SHELLS -- five shells, and which one to takeIf you are copying part of this collection onto your own media you want ONEshell, and probably two: one to type at and one for programs to shell outthrough.  This file exists so you do not have to install all five to find outhow they differ.Short answer: take `bash' to type at, and `ksh' as well if anything you useshells out -- which is more programs than you would guess.  Take `sh' aloneonly if you need the smallest real Bourne shell and will not run anythingthat calls system().  `gshell' and `mshell' are not command shells at all;they are full-screen menus, and they are worth having for that.THE FIVE--------  file      bytes   needs   module   source            docs                     cio     name  ------------------------------------------------------------------------  bash     242604    no     bash     -                 DOC/bash (3 files)  sh        77306    no     sh       -                 DOC/sh (4 files)  ksh      118554    YES    ksh      SRC/pdksh (171)   DOC/ksh, DOC/pdksh,                                                       DOC/README-KSH  gshell    23114    no     gshell   SRC/eff_gshell    DOC/gshell (4 files)  mshell     7228    YES    mshell   -                 DOC/mshell  bash    GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12 -- this disk's own, and what SYS/login          hands you  sh      Bourne shell v7.5, Martin Gregorie's sh75 package  ksh     pdksh, the public-domain Korn shell  gshell  GSHELL V1.1, Uwe Simon 1988 -- a menu  mshell  a menu shell driven by a menu fileWHAT $SHELL ACTUALLY DOES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT------------------------------------------------Until 2026-09-11 this section said $SHELL is what lets programs shell out,and that TeX and eo do nothing without it.  Both claims were wrong.  They arereplaced here by measurement.Microware's C library system() FORKS THE BARE MODULE NAME `shell'.  It doesnot read $SHELL.  Traced with `os9exec -d 0x0002', F$Fork is handed thestring "shell" from a compiled-in constant and answers E_MNF, because nothingon this disk is called `shell' -- the five here are bash, ksh, sh, gshell andmshell.  Nothing runs, and THE RETURN VALUE TELLS YOU NOTHING: measured hereit was a junk address that moved between runs, and measured on another machinethe same failure returned 0, which reads as success.  Do not test it.  SHELLset to ksh, to sh, to Microware's own shell, or left unset entirely, all givethe identical result.The sound test is whether a REDIRECT TARGET WAS CREATED.  A shell makes thefile named after `>' before it runs the command, so `prog > out' in a writabledirectory leaves `out' behind even when `prog' does not exist.  No file at allmeans no shell ever read the string.ON A REAL OS-9 SYSTEM THIS NEVER ARISES.  /dd/CMDS/shell is Microware's ownand is always present.  It is a gap in this collection standing by itself,which is the arrangement you get when you try it under an emulator.SO SYS/login LOADS YOUR OWN SHELL, IF YOU HAVE ONE.  It makes `shell'resident along with runb and the other commands of yours that programs hererun by name, each as    load /h1/CMDS/<name> >/nil 2>/nilwhich takes the shell off YOUR system, mounted as /h1, and makes it residentunder the name system() looks for.  Nothing of Microware's is shipped here tomake that work -- it is your own licensed copy, loaded from your own disk.With no /h1 each load finds nothing and moves on, and programs that shell out go back to doing nothing.One difference worth knowing if you compare the two shells.  Microware'sshell wants its own redirection syntax, and a program that builds a Unix-styleline for system() may hand it something it will not take:    cat file > out     `^syntax error', and system() RETURNS 0 ANYWAY    cat file >out      worksSo a redirect with a space before the `>' fails silently -- the command doesnot run and the caller is told it succeeded.  ksh accepts both spellings.Neither shell rescues everything: `ed's `!' fails earlier for its own reason,and RCS `ci' builds `>-tmpfile', which is Microware redirection no shell hereparses.What is affected, measured program by program:  m4         syscmd is SILENT without a `shell' module and works with one.             The only shipped program found to be affected by this.  ed         calls system() at SRC/ed/main.c:1014, but its `!' escape fails             earlier and for its own reason: SRC/ed/buf.c opens a scratch             file as /r0/ed.XXXXXX, and that fails first.  gawk       refuses system() outright -- the OS-9 port reports "the system             function is not supported".  make       unaffected.  It calls os9exec() directly and prints the command             line as it runs it.  tex        unaffected, and so are latex, eo and maketexpk.  All four give             identical output with SHELL set, with SHELL unset, and with or             without a `shell' module present.ksh is still the right thing for SHELL to name, and SYS/login still sets it:bash reads it, and a program that examines $SHELL itself then finds a shellthat can parse a command line.  Handed `/dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd' as aSINGLE argument, measured 2026-08-31:  ksh      40 /dd/SYS/motd                      it parses it.  WORKS  bash     "/dd/CMDS/wc: cannot execute binary file"           -- bash reads the argument as a SCRIPT FILENAME  sh       "/dd/CMDS/wc: file not found"           -- sh cannot fork an absolute pathname here  gshell   opens its menu with `Directory : /dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd'  mshell   "Too many items in /dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd (> 10)"The four `F$SetSys: unimplemented 03D8' lines os9exec prints when ksh startsare noise from the emulator, not an error.bash -- the one to type at--------------------------  GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12.  Command history, job control, functions,  and scripts that come across from Unix unchanged.  It is the shell  SYS/login exec's, and /dd/.bashrc is read because login points HOME at  this disk.  TWO OF ITS BUILT-INS ARE REPLACED IN .bashrc AND MUST STAY REPLACED.  bash's own `pwd' HANGS the shell and, where /dd is not an RBF device, its  `cd' aborts with a bus error: getwd() walks `..' upward looking for a  single root, and OS-9 has one root per device, so the walk never ends.  /dd/.bashrc replaces both with functions that track the path as a string.  A consequence worth knowing before you write a test: bash's `cd' does not  move the OS-9 DATA directory.  `cd somewhere; prog' runs prog with the  data directory wherever it already was, so a program that opens a file by  bare name will not find it where you think.  Costs: the largest here by a factor of two, and it cannot serve as $SHELL.sh -- the small real Bourne shell----------------------------------  Bourne shell v7.5 from Martin Gregorie's sh75 package -- the same package  `cat', `basename' and `dirname' came from.  A third of bash's size, needs  no Microware module, and it has a real `chd': `pwd' inside it reports a  directory that actually moved, which bash cannot do.  Costs, and they are severe here: it CANNOT FORK A PROGRAM BY ABSOLUTE  PATHNAME under os9exec.  `sh -c '/dd/CMDS/wc -c file'` answers `file not  found' for a file that plainly exists, with PATH set and `chx' done.  So  it cannot launch most of this collection, and it cannot be $SHELL.  Take it if you want the smallest genuine Bourne shell and a working `chd',  and you will run programs by short name from a directory on PATH.ksh -- the one programs need-----------------------------  pdksh, the public-domain Korn shell, whole source in SRC/pdksh (171 files)  and its manual page in DOC/ksh.  It is the only shell here that takes a command line as a single argument,  which is what makes it the right $SHELL.  `ksh -c '<commands>'` works too.  Its INTERACTIVE loop depends on the emulator: it reads a command line with  read(fd, buf, 256), and an os9exec whose I$Read waited for the full count  rather than returning at the end-of-record character never saw a typed  command.  With that corrected it is a full shell -- prompt, for loops,  variables, forking.  DOC/README-KSH is the whole story.  Two faults in its `read', measured 2026-09-27, for anyone scripting it:  only the first `while read ...; done < file' in a script reads its  file -- a later one gets end-of-file at once, because stdin keeps the  first one's end-of-file -- and a run of spaces between words is taken  as several separators, so `read a b c' on `x   y' leaves b empty.  `line=...; set -- $line' splits properly; CMDS/man shows both ways  round.  sh and bash read correctly.  Costs: it needs Microware's `cio', which ships here with Microware's  permission but which you may not have on your own machine.  And it makes  os9exec print four `F$SetSys: unimplemented' lines when it starts.gshell and mshell -- menus, not shells---------------------------------------  Neither reads commands from a keyboard the way the other three do.  `gshell' draws a full-screen file browser: a lettered list of the current  directory, `+' and `-' to page, `.' to change directory, a letter to run  a file and its lower-case twin to run it with parameters.  Three  front-ends on this disk drive the same engine -- `assembler', `compiler'  and `editor' -- and each is that menu pointed at one job.  Uwe Simon,  1988; source in SRC/eff_gshell, help text in DOC/gshell.  `mshell' is smaller still and reads a MENU FILE named on its command  line, saying `Could not open <name> (menufile)' without one.  Both want  TERM set, as every full-screen program here does.  Take one of these if you want to hand somebody a machine that runs four  things and nothing else.  Do not take one expecting to type at it.CHOOSING--------  You want a shell to work in                            bash  You want $SHELL to name a shell that can parse         ksh  You want the smallest real Bourne shell                sh  You want a real `chd' that moves the data directory    sh  You have no cio and cannot get one                     bash or sh  You want a menu for somebody else to use               gshell  If you take exactly one file: `bash'.  If you take two, `bash' and `ksh',  and set SHELL to the ksh -- SYS/login on this disk shows the line.THE WEB SERVER IS THE SHARPEST CASE OF THIS-------------------------------------------  WN runs a CGI program through the C library's system(), which forks the  shell, so with SHELL unset every CGI request answers      HTTP/1.0 500 Can't exec CGI program  and nothing else says why.  The failure is convincing: the CGI module  runs perfectly when you type its name, its attributes are right, and its  entry in index.cache is right.  Measured 2026-09-23 on WN's own examples  under c/unid/wn_1.14.3/osk/EXAMPLES -- with SHELL set the same request  returns 200 and the program's output.A SCRIPT AT THE END OF A PIPE WAITS FOR EVER--------------------------------------------      cat notes | bash myfilter         never finishes      cat notes | myprogram             finishes  A shell whose standard input is a pipe keeps that path open while it  waits for the command it started.  OS-9's pipe manager ends a read with  end-of-file only when the pipe is empty and every process holding the  path is reading it; the waiting shell holds it without reading, so the  command inside the script never sees end-of-file.  Every shell here does  this, Microware's included.  It is the pipe manager's documented rule,  observed on os9exec and not yet checked on hardware.  Give the script  the file as an argument instead, or run the program itself in the pipe.